# generic-inject-example — system overview
# A worked example of injecting a Python interpreter into a running Windows
# game process and calling one engine function from Python code.
# Zones run left to right: entry points -> core -> state -> external.
# Style classes set strokes only, so light and dark stay readable.

title: |md
  # generic-inject-example
  Injects CPython into a live Soldier of Fortune process, runs a Python payload inside it, and streams that payload's output back to the terminal.
| {near: top-center}

direction: right
grid-columns: 2
grid-rows: 2
horizontal-gap: 110
vertical-gap: 100

entry: Entry points {
  direction: down

  test: `test.py` · driver: process name in, Python injected, payload output streamed back
  launch: `launch.sh` · git-bash wrapper that calls `cmd.exe /C python.exe test.py NAME PATH`
  stub: the stub template · Python source, kept as a string in `test.py`: open the pipe, redirect stdout and stderr, run the payload as `__mayhem__`
  payload: `script.py` · the example payload: call engine `COM_Printf` at 0x2001C6E0, then print
  orig: `orig.py` · upstream `python_injector.py` untouched, kept as the diff baseline
  deps: `Pipfile` · python 3.8 plus boltons, the one third-party package
}

core: Core {
  direction: down

  find: `getpid.py` · `EnumProcesses` then `GetProcessImageFileNameA`, first basename match wins
  open: `WindowsProcess(pid=...)` · mayhem handle: read and write memory, allocate, remote threads
  dll: locate `pythonXY.dll` · scanned out of `sys.path` by filename, not `find_library`
  load: `LoadLibrary` in the target · by absolute path, so the target must read it and match bitness
  rva: resolve two exports · `Py_InitializeEx` and `PyRun_SimpleString` as remote base plus local delta
  init: start the interpreter · `Py_InitializeEx(0)` in a remote thread, then join
  run: run the stub · `PyRun_SimpleString` on the stub string copied into the target
  pipe: named pipe server · duplex, message mode, 255 instances, blocking connect
  rel: stream and tear down · echo until the pipe breaks, join, re-init, free the dll

  find -> open -> load -> rva -> init -> run
  dll -> load: one path, one bitness
  run -> pipe: the stub connects back
  pipe -> rel: output until EOF
}

state: State {
  direction: down

  none: nothing is written · no config, no log, no cache · the payload path comes from argv
  src: `script.py` on disk · the target opens it by absolute path, so the game must be able to read it
  buf: the named pipe · the only channel out, anything not written to it is lost {shape: cylinder}
}

ext: External {
  direction: down

  game: the target process · SoF 1.06 in practice, it owns 0x2001C6E0 and executes the injected code
  py: `pythonXY.dll` · must match the driver's major.minor and the game's bitness
  mayhem: vendored `mayhem` 0.5.0 · win32 ctypes bindings, BSD-3, untouched subtree
  syscalls: `windows_syscalls.db` · 39,761 syscall numbers shipped with mayhem, unused here
}

entry -> core: the driver wires the three pieces together
core -> state: nothing persisted, one pipe carries the output
core -> ext: loads a dll into another process and calls its exports

classes: {
  entry: {style: {stroke: "#2f6fbf"}}
  core: {style: {stroke: "#2f8f5f"}}
  state: {style: {stroke: "#a86a12"; stroke-dash: 3}}
  ext: {style: {stroke: "#8a4fbf"; stroke-dash: 5}}
}

entry.class: entry
core.class: core
state.class: state
ext.class: ext
